    <!-- Google Analytics (GA4). ONE gtag loader, TWO GA4 properties:
           1. G-JK97LLP97M â€” the standalone web property.
           2. VITE_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID â€” the Firebase project's GA4
              property, the one the iOS/Android apps report to.
         gtag.js fans every `event` out to every configured measurement id,
         so both properties receive identical page_view/sign_up/... traffic
         and the Firebase console (which reads from property 2) keeps
         web/mobile parity. The Firebase Analytics JS SDK used to be loaded
         for property 2; it is just GA4 under the hood (it fetched the web
         config, created a Firebase installation, then injected a second
         gtag/js?id=<measurementId> script â€” a 1.9 s chain and a duplicate
         gtag download on mobile), so it was removed in favour of the extra
         `config` line below. The only loss is the Firebase app-instance id
         for web visitors, i.e. Firebase audiences keyed on it.

         The Firebase id is substituted at build time by Vite's HTML env
         replacement (percent-wrapped VITE_* names). When the var is
         unset (local dev) Vite leaves the placeholder in place, so the
         regex guard skips the second config rather than registering a
         bogus id.

         The router-aware tracker in src/lib/analytics.ts fires page_view on
         every SPA route change without a `send_to`, so it reaches both
         properties; gtag's automatic send_page_view is disabled on each so
         initial + subsequent views don't double-count.

         The stub below stays synchronous so `js` + both `config` entries are
         the first dataLayer entries and every later gtag()/dataLayer.push()
         queues behind them. gtag.js itself is injected after the window
         `load` event (it used to be an async <script> in <head>, which
         competed with the entry chunk and hero images on mobile); when it
         arrives it drains the queue in order, so no event is lost â€” only
         delivered later. -->
    

